WEBVTT

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.006
You ask an AI a simple question.

00:00:03.006 --> 00:00:04.638
The answer comes back polished,

00:00:04.638 --> 00:00:06.617
friendly, and completely sure.

00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:08.443
Here’s a made-up example.

00:00:08.443 --> 00:00:11.315
Is the Willowmere Museum
open on Mondays?

00:00:11.315 --> 00:00:13.626
“Yes! It’s open daily, nine to five.

00:00:13.626 --> 00:00:15.479
And entry is free on Mondays.”

00:00:15.659 --> 00:00:17.135
Now let’s check each claim

00:00:17.135 --> 00:00:19.291
against the museum’s own website.

00:00:19.291 --> 00:00:21.362
Nine to five. That matches.

00:00:21.362 --> 00:00:22.426
Open daily? No.

00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:24.456
It’s closed on Mondays.

00:00:24.456 --> 00:00:25.301
Free on Mondays?

00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:27.708
The website doesn’t say that anywhere.

00:00:27.928 --> 00:00:29.870
So why did it sound so sure?

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.221
Behind the answer
is a language model.

00:00:32.221 --> 00:00:34.662
It writes by predicting
likely next words,

00:00:34.662 --> 00:00:37.231
from patterns in
huge amounts of text.

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:41.251
Phrases like “open daily” appear
on countless opening-hours pages,

00:00:41.251 --> 00:00:42.646
so the words fit.

00:00:42.646 --> 00:00:44.632
The same process got the hours right,

00:00:44.632 --> 00:00:45.839
and the day wrong.

00:00:45.839 --> 00:00:48.852
Sounding right and being checked
are different jobs.

00:00:49.032 --> 00:00:51.882
And the way models are often
trained and tested

00:00:51.882 --> 00:00:53.544
can reward a confident guess

00:00:53.544 --> 00:00:55.606
over saying, “I’m not sure.”

00:00:55.786 --> 00:00:57.080
Search and links can help,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.900
but they aren’t proof on their own.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.091
A cited page may not say
what the answer claimed.

00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:03.772
So here’s the habit.

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:05.070
Ask for the source.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:05.980
Open it.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.968
Find the sentence that
actually supports the claim.

00:01:09.148 --> 00:01:11.145
Confidence is a tone of voice.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:13.300
Evidence is something you can check.
